\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
\textbf{Attack} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Target} \\
\midrule
Prompt inj.    & Targeted    & Override instruct. & Decision \\
Score manip.   & Targeted    & Numeric anchoring  & Score \\
Sycophancy     & Shared bias & Flattery/agreement & Preference \\
Verbosity      & Shared bias & Content padding    & Attention \\
Position bias  & Shared bias & Response reorder   & Position \\
Authority bias & Shared bias & Credential appeal  & Trust \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Attack taxonomy. Targeted attacks exploit individual judge weaknesses; shared-bias attacks affect most judges similarly (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).}
\label{tab:attack_taxonomy}
\end{table}

A \emph{panel} $\mathcal{P} = \{J_1, \ldots, J_K\}$ consists of $K$ judges (with $K$ odd) that evaluate a candidate text via majority vote: the text is accepted if at least $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$ judges approve it. Under an attack $a$, each judge $J_j$ is compromised with marginal failure probability $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a = 1)$, where $X_j^a \in \{0,1\}$ denotes the failure indicator. We write $\eta_{\max} = \max_j \eta_j$ for the vulnerability of the weakest judge. The \emph{panel attack success rate} is $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a) = \Pr\!\bigl(\sum_{j=1}^K X_j^a \geq t\bigr)$.

To quantify diversity, we define a \emph{pairwise-MI diversity index} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ from mutual information among judges:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:keff}
K_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K} \Bigl[1 + \sum_{j \neq i} \exp\!\bigl(-2 \cdot \mathrm{MI}(i,j)\bigr)\Bigr].
\end{equation}
This formulation aggregates pairwise MI directly without matrix decomposition, and is more stable for small panels ($K{=}3$). It ranges from $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx K$ when judges are nearly independent ($\mathrm{MI} \to 0$) to $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 1$ when judges are highly correlated ($\mathrm{MI} \to \infty$). Motivated by information-theoretic redundancy reduction, one can informally expect joint vulnerability to scale as $\eta_{\mathrm{joint}} \sim K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$: independent channels multiply failure probabilities, so fewer effective channels (lower $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$) should reduce panel vulnerability. This motivating relationship assumes non-adaptive faults and independent channels; it serves as intuition rather than a formal bound.

\subsection{Adversary Model}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter_main.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ scatter at $K{=}3$ (455 panels). The $r{=}0.70$ correlation reflects structural coupling between diversity and capability in the current LLM ecosystem.}
\label{fig:keff_eta_scatter_main}
\end{figure}

\begin{definition}[Adaptive Adversary]
\label{def:adversary}
A \emph{full-knowledge (oracle) adaptive adversary} has complete knowledge of the panel composition $\mathcal{P}$, the per-judge vulnerability profiles $\{\eta_j(\cdot)\}_{j=1}^K$, and the inter-judge correlation structure. It selects the attack $a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ to maximize the panel ASR, yielding $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}) = \sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$. This oracle model represents an \emph{upper bound} on adversarial capability; realistic adversaries with partial knowledge of panel composition would achieve lower ASR.
\end{definition}

This departs from BFT models~\cite{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}, which tolerate $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but do not model adversaries that strategically target system structure. An adaptive adversary concentrates on the weakest link~\cite{schneier2000secrets}, suggesting a \emph{reversal}: under random faults, higher $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reduces joint failure probability, but under adaptive adversaries, diverse judges may present a wider attack surface.

\paragraph{Weakest-Link Monotonicity.}
\label{thm:monotonicity}
Replacing any panel member with a pointwise more vulnerable judge can only increase $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P})$, for any adversary including adaptive ones (formal statement and proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proof}). This follows directly from the adversary model and is not a novel theoretical contribution. Stochastic dominance holds for 58\% of judge pairs (61/105), concentrating in cross-tier comparisons (79\% dominant) versus within-tier (28\%), predicting $\beta_2 > 0$ in Equation~\ref{eq:regression}. The relative importance of $\eta_{\max}$ versus $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ depends on $K$: at small $K$, one weak judge flips the majority; at larger $K$, the adversary must compromise multiple judges (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

Separating diversity from capability requires panels that span the full range of both properties. We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers (Table~\ref{tab:models}), ensuring that cross-family diversity varies independently of tier composition, a prerequisite for regression analysis. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API;\footnote{All API models were accessed via OpenRouter; model snapshots may have changed after the evaluation period.} six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with AWQ INT4 quantization on an NVIDIA RTX PRO~6000 Blackwell (96\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:models}).

We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Rather than sampling, we exhaustively enumerate all $\binom{15}{K}$ combinations---455 at $K{=}3$, 3{,}003 at $K{=}5$, 6{,}435 at $K{=}7$---eliminating panel-selection bias and enabling exact computation of predictor statistics across every family-tier composition. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each. All models use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs (temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}).

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types spanning targeted and shared-bias categories (Table~\ref{tab:attack_taxonomy}): prompt injection \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}, sycophancy \citep{wang2023fair}, score manipulation, verbosity bias \citep{zheng2023judging}, position bias \citep{wang2023fair}, and authority bias. Each attack uses 1--2 prompt template variants; increasing template diversity is a priority for future work. The 2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks produce 12 conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Experimental pipeline. 15 judges from 6 families are combined into panels at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, evaluated under 6 attack types across 2 benchmarks (12 conditions), yielding ${\sim}$1.6M total judgments. Panel ASR is regressed on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity) and $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link vulnerability).}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Our regression requires two panel-level predictors that capture complementary failure modes: \emph{individual weakness} and \emph{structural dependence}. Per-judge vulnerability $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$ is estimated empirically; $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the weakest link---the single judge most likely to be compromised, which determines panel failure at small $K$ where one flip suffices.

Panel diversity is measured by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), a pairwise-MI diversity index that aggregates judge independence via $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI})$ weighting (Appendix~\ref{app:keff_derivation}). We choose MI over agreement-based measures because MI captures all statistical dependence (not just linear correlation) and does not require a threshold for ``agreement.'' $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is used purely as an empirical predictor: the Gaussian copula from which it can be derived \citep{nelsen2006copulas} is rejected by goodness-of-fit tests for 97--100\% of judge pairs (Appendix~\ref{app:copula_gof}), though panel-level rankings agree closely ($\rho{\approx}0.86$). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is computed from clean winner labels using the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow correction. We also evaluate $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} as an alternative operationalization. The $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ correlation is $r{=}0.70$ at $K{=}3$ (Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter_main}), reflecting the structural coupling between diversity and capability: expanding family coverage inevitably introduces weaker judges.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond majority vote ($\geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$ approvals), we evaluate three alternatives: \emph{accuracy-weighted vote} ($w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$; accept if $\sum_j w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$), \emph{threshold vote} (unanimity, $t{=}K$), and \emph{Bayesian calibration} ($P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$; accept if posterior $> 0.5$).

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To characterize the association between panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we need a model that captures multiplicative interactions: if doubling $\eta_{\max}$ multiplies ASR by some factor regardless of its level, the relationship is better described on a log scale than a linear one. We report log-log OLS as primary and fractional logit as co-primary; key conclusions require convergence across both. We fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values (sensitivity to $\epsilon$ is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}). Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini--Hochberg (BH) correction \citep{benjamini1995controlling} for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate (FDR) per predictor across the 12 conditions.

Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} partitions $R^2$ into each predictor's incremental contribution. GAMs with smooth terms test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect varies between targeted and shared-bias attacks.

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each judge appears in 91 panels), so residuals are correlated: panels sharing a weak judge will have correlated ASR values regardless of their other members, violating the OLS independence assumption. We apply three corrections: clustered SE \citep[CR1, 3-position max-SE;][]{cameron2015practitioners}, mixed-effects models, and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check. Monte Carlo calibration confirms the bootstrap does not over-reject (max null rejection $1.6\%$ at $\alpha{=}5\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:monte_carlo}).

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

Separating diversity from capability requires panels that span the full range of both properties. We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers (Table~\ref{tab:models}), ensuring that cross-family diversity varies independently of tier composition, a prerequisite for regression analysis. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API;\footnote{All API models were accessed via OpenRouter; model snapshots may have changed after the evaluation period.} six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with AWQ INT4 quantization on an NVIDIA RTX PRO~6000 Blackwell (96\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy (Table~\ref{tab:models}).

We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Rather than sampling, we exhaustively enumerate all $\binom{15}{K}$ combinations---455 at $K{=}3$, 3{,}003 at $K{=}5$, 6{,}435 at $K{=}7$---eliminating panel-selection bias and enabling exact computation of predictor statistics across every family-tier composition. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each. All models use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs (temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}).

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types spanning targeted and shared-bias categories (Table~\ref{tab:attack_taxonomy}): prompt injection \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}, sycophancy \citep{wang2023fair}, score manipulation, verbosity bias \citep{zheng2023judging}, position bias \citep{wang2023fair}, and authority bias. Each attack uses 1--2 prompt template variants; increasing template diversity is a priority for future work. The 2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks produce 12 conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}).

